.TH BT 2
.SH NAME
bt, h4, btusb, hci, l2cap, att, gatt, smp \- Bluetooth Low Energy protocol modules
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "bt.m";
bt   := load Bt Bt->PATH;
t    := load Bttransport Bttransport->H4PATH;
hci  := load Hci Hci->PATH;
l2   := load L2cap L2cap->PATH;
att  := load Att Att->PATH;
gatt := load Gatt Gatt->PATH;
smp  := load Smp Smp->PATH;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are the layers of the Bluetooth Low Energy stack that
.IR btfs (4)
serves as a name space.  A program that wants the name space should
use
.I btfs
and never these; they are described because
.I btfs
is one way to arrange them and not the only one, and because the
division between them is the part worth keeping.
.PP
Each is loaded and then given the layer below it, so a stack is built
from the bottom up: a transport, then
.BR Hci ,
then
.B L2cap
on a connection,
.B Att
on a channel of that, and
.B Gatt
or
.B Smp
above.  Everything is little endian on the wire and
.B Bt
holds the accessors and the spellings \- addresses and UUIDs written
most significant byte first, controller error codes as text.
.TF Bttransport
.PD
.TP
.B Bt
The shared accessors: the HCI packet indicators, byte order,
addresses, hexadecimal, UUIDs, opcodes and error strings.
.TP
.B Bttransport
One whole HCI packet in each direction, and nothing else.  Two
implementations are built and they are interchangeable:
.B H4PATH
frames packets on a byte stream, which is what a UART-attached
controller speaks and what a
.IR dial (2)
string reaches; and
.B USBPATH
carries them on the endpoints of a USB Bluetooth controller (written,
never demonstrated \- see
.IR btfs (4)).
Nothing above this interface knows which it has.
.TP
.B Hci
The controller.  Commands are serialised, because a controller answers
one at a time; events are parsed and dispatched; ACL data is
fragmented going down and reassembled coming up; and the controller's
ACL buffers are accounted, so a write waits rather than overruns.  It
also does LE scanning, connection setup and teardown, and the
commands SMP needs.
.TP
.B L2cap
Length and CID framing over an ACL connection, a queue per CID, and
the LE signalling channel.  On an LE link the channels are fixed:
.B Cattr
for the attribute protocol,
.B Cle
for signalling,
.B Csmp
for security management.  There are no connection-oriented channels.
.TP
.B Att
The attribute protocol: one outstanding request at a time, answered or
refused with an error naming the handle, and notifications and
indications arriving in between on their own queue.
.TP
.B Gatt
Services, characteristics and descriptors, discovered by three
loops over
.BR Att ,
and reading, writing and subscribing to a characteristic.
.TP
.B Smp
LE legacy pairing as the initiator.  Its key functions
.B c1
and
.B s1
are built out of AES-128, so it needs only
.IR crypt (2)
and never asks the controller to do arithmetic.
.PD
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/bt
.br
.B /module/bt.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR btfs (4),
.IR crypt (2),
.IR dial (2)
.SH BUGS
.PP
LE only, initiator only, and pairing is Just Works.  What that means,
and what is therefore not protected, is set out in
.IR btfs (4).
